          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-d3e14cfbfa1e549d\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Adolfo’s retrospective “ti ho amata davvero, onestamente” (“I truly, honestly loved you”) explicitly presents his past love for Loredana as true. It makes no present relational undertaking, and its force is controlled avowal used within an accusation rather than expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"“Onestamente” may suggest that Adolfo regards his former love or courtship as honorable, but it does not independently enact a commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Adolfo directly addresses Loredana with a first-person, past-tense avowal embedded in his accusatory speech.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Adolfo invokes his former genuine and honorable love to claim personal standing while accusing and humiliating Loredana. The narration frames the speech as deliberately cruel: he is driven to trample and destroy his love and takes satisfaction in her defenselessness, before remorse follows. This weaponized and possibly self-conflicted use affects the interpersonal meaning but does not alter the core utterance’s dominant truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“a me, che ti ho amata davvero, onestamente” (“to me, who truly, honestly loved you”)\",\"supports\":\"The perfect-tense clause explicitly avows a past loving state, with adverbs emphasizing its claimed genuineness and honorable character.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator describes Adolfo as driven by a need “di calpestare e di distruggere il suo amore” and as taking pleasure in tormenting his former fiancée.\",\"supports\":\"The love avowal is being used as an accusatory weapon in calculated cruelty, not produced as reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After Loredana leaves, Adolfo is suddenly overcome by remorse.\",\"supports\":\"His later remorse reinforces the conflicted and cruel contextual framing without creating performative or expressive force in the love clause itself.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative perfect form, syntactically main or embedded, and reviewed as a valid explicit I–you love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the target is a retrospective explicit love predication rather than a nonsemantic or merely metalinguistic token.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the retrospective avowal. Adolfo’s cruelty, rhetorical self-positioning, and conflicted motives belong to contextual interpretation; they create no residual core force requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
